/-!
# A ring module-finite over a semilocal ring is semilocal

Tag 0C0T's elementary route trivialises an invertible module near a point `t` of `Y ∖ V` by
passing to `X ×_Y Spec 𝒪_{Y,t}`, which is the spectrum of a ring module-finite over the local
ring `𝒪_{Y,t}`, and invoking the triviality of the Picard group of a semilocal ring. Two inputs
that step needs have been recorded as absent from the library and from Mathlib; the first of
them is

> nothing in the library or in Mathlib says a ring module-finite over a semilocal ring is
> semilocal (`Finite (MaximalSpectrum ·)` has instances in Mathlib only for Artinian rings, for a
> fraction ring of itself, and in one localization lemma)

and this file supplies it. The absence claim is correct about instances and about the statement;
what it misses is that the two ingredients the statement needs are both in pinned Mathlib and
nobody had composed them, which is the composition-gap shape rather than missing mathematics.

## Main results

All names below live in the namespace `Stacks.RingTheory.SemilocalModuleFinite`.

* `finite_maximalSpectrum_of_moduleFinite`: if `S` is module-finite over `R` and `R` has finitely
  many maximal ideals, then so does `S`. This is "semilocal" in pinned Mathlib's spelling,
  `Finite (MaximalSpectrum ·)`, and no Noetherian, Artinian or domain hypothesis is used.
* `finite_maximalSpectrum_of_moduleFinite_of_isLocalRing`: the case Tag 0C0T's route actually
  reaches, where the base is the local ring of `Y` at a point.
* `nonempty_linearEquiv_self_of_moduleFinite`: the conclusion the route wants — an invertible
  module over a ring module-finite over a semilocal ring is isomorphic to the ring.
* `nonempty_linearEquiv_self_of_moduleFinite_of_isLocalRing`: the same over a local base.
* `exists_mem_primeCompl_forall_exists_smul_eq`: the spreading-out of the previous item — an
  invertible module over an algebra module-finite over `A` is generated by a single element
  after inverting one element of `A` outside a given prime.

## The two ingredients, and why neither alone is the statement

The proof is that the contraction map on maximal spectra has finite source *because* it has finite
target and finite fibres:

* **Finite target.** For `S` integral over `R` the contraction `𝔪 ↦ 𝔪 ∩ R` of a maximal ideal is
  maximal — `Ideal.IsMaximal.under`, an instance, with `Algebra.IsIntegral R S` supplied by
  `Algebra.IsIntegral.of_finite` from module-finiteness. So the map lands in `MaximalSpectrum R`,
  which is finite by hypothesis.
* **Finite fibres.** The fibre over `p` is contained in `p.primesOver S`, the primes of `S` lying
  over `p`, and that set is finite for a quasi-finite algebra:
  `Algebra.QuasiFinite.finite_primesOver`. Module-finiteness gives `Algebra.QuasiFinite R S` by a
  low-priority instance in `Mathlib/RingTheory/QuasiFinite/Basic.lean`.

Neither ingredient is the statement. Mathlib's own nearest result,
`Algebra.QuasiFinite.finite_primeSpectrum`, concludes `Finite (PrimeSpectrum S)` and *assumes*
`Finite (PrimeSpectrum R)` — a hypothesis strictly stronger than semilocality, which the local
ring `𝒪_{Y,t}` of Tag 0C0T's route does not satisfy in general, so it cannot be used here. What is
special about the maximal spectrum is exactly the first ingredient: the contraction of a maximal
ideal along an integral extension stays maximal, which is false for the prime spectrum.

## What this does and does not do for Tag 0C0T

With this, `CommRing.Pic.instSubsingletonPic` applies to the ring presenting
`X ×_Y Spec 𝒪_{Y,t}`, so the Picard group there vanishes and every invertible module over it is
free: that is `nonempty_linearEquiv_self_of_moduleFinite_of_isLocalRing`. The step then needs
only the second missing input, which this file does not supply and which is restated here: **nothing
identifies an invertible module sheaf on `Spec R` with an invertible
`R`-module**, so the invertible `𝒪_X`-module of the tag cannot yet be fed to the statements below,
and the passage from a trivialization over the limit `X ×_Y Spec 𝒪_{Y,t}` to one over a finite
stage `f⁻¹(U_t)` is a spreading-out that this file does not address either. No `stacks` attribute
is attached: Tag 0C0T carries a single unlabelled assertion, nothing of it is proved here, and
these are statements of commutative algebra that the tag does not assert.

The *algebraic* half of the second absence is now supplied by
`exists_mem_primeCompl_forall_exists_smul_eq`: the passage from a trivialization over
`Spec 𝒪_{A,p}` to one over a basic open of `Spec A` is there, for an invertible module over an
algebra module-finite over `A`.  What the tag's route still needs on top of it is the
identification of an invertible module sheaf on `Spec R` with an invertible `R`-module.

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0C0T](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0C0T)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 00GS](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/00GS)
-/

@[expose] public section

universe u v

namespace Stacks.RingTheory.SemilocalModuleFinite

open Ideal

variable (R : Type u) (S : Type v) [CommRing R] [CommRing S] [Algebra R S]

/-- **A ring module-finite over a semilocal ring is semilocal.** If `S` is a module-finite
`R`-algebra and `R` has only finitely many maximal ideals, then `S` has only finitely many maximal
ideals.

The contraction `MaximalSpectrum S → MaximalSpectrum R` is well defined because `S` is integral
over `R` (`Ideal.IsMaximal.under`), and each of its fibres is contained in the set of primes of
`S` lying over the corresponding maximal ideal of `R`, which is finite because a module-finite
algebra is quasi-finite (`Algebra.QuasiFinite.finite_primesOver`). A type with a map to a finite
type all of whose fibres are finite is finite.

No Noetherian, Artinian or domain hypothesis is needed, and the conclusion is `Finite`, not
`Fintype`: no bound on the number of maximal ideals of `S` is produced. -/
@[ega "0 22.3.1" "semilocality of A"]
theorem finite_maximalSpectrum_of_moduleFinite
    [Module.Finite R S] [Finite (MaximalSpectrum R)] :
    Finite (MaximalSpectrum S) := by
  classical
  -- The fibre of the contraction map over `p`, as a set of maximal ideals of `S`.
  set fibre : MaximalSpectrum R → Set (MaximalSpectrum S) :=
    fun p => {I : MaximalSpectrum S | I.asIdeal.under R = p.asIdeal} with hfibre
  have hfin : ∀ p : MaximalSpectrum R, (fibre p).Finite := by
    intro p
    have hinj : Set.InjOn (fun I : MaximalSpectrum S => I.asIdeal) (fibre p) := by
      intro a _ b _ hab
      exact MaximalSpectrum.ext hab
    have himg : (fun I : MaximalSpectrum S => I.asIdeal) '' fibre p ⊆
        p.asIdeal.primesOver S := by
      rintro J ⟨I, hI, rfl⟩
      exact ⟨I.isMaximal.isPrime, ⟨hI.symm⟩⟩
    exact Set.Finite.of_finite_image
      ((Algebra.QuasiFinite.finite_primesOver p.asIdeal).subset himg) hinj
  have hcover : (Set.univ : Set (MaximalSpectrum S)) ⊆ ⋃ p, fibre p := by
    intro I _
    exact Set.mem_iUnion.mpr ⟨⟨I.asIdeal.under R, inferInstance⟩, rfl⟩
  exact Set.finite_univ_iff.mp ((Set.finite_iUnion hfin).subset hcover)

/-- A local ring has finitely many maximal ideals. -/
private theorem finite_maximalSpectrum_of_isLocalRing [IsLocalRing R] :
    Finite (MaximalSpectrum R) :=
  have : Subsingleton (MaximalSpectrum R) :=
    ⟨fun a b => MaximalSpectrum.ext
      ((IsLocalRing.eq_maximalIdeal a.isMaximal).trans
        (IsLocalRing.eq_maximalIdeal b.isMaximal).symm)⟩
  Finite.of_subsingleton

/-- **A ring module-finite over a local ring is semilocal.** The case of
`finite_maximalSpectrum_of_moduleFinite` that Tag 0C0T's route reaches, with the base the local
ring of `Y` at a point of `Y ∖ V`: a local ring has exactly one maximal ideal, hence finitely
many. -/
@[ega "0 10.3.1.2" "partial",
  ega "0 22.3.1" "special case",
  ega "0 23.2.5" "semi-local clause",
  ega "IV 5.4.3" "semilocality", sga "1 III 1.4" "semilocality of B ⊗_A A′"]
theorem finite_maximalSpectrum_of_moduleFinite_of_isLocalRing
    [IsLocalRing R] [Module.Finite R S] :
    Finite (MaximalSpectrum S) := by
  have := finite_maximalSpectrum_of_isLocalRing R
  exact finite_maximalSpectrum_of_moduleFinite R S

/-- **An invertible module over a ring module-finite over a semilocal ring is isomorphic to the
ring.** Composing `finite_maximalSpectrum_of_moduleFinite` with the triviality of the Picard group
of a semilocal ring (`CommRing.Pic.instSubsingletonPic`) and freeness of an invertible module over
a ring with trivial Picard group (`CommRing.Pic.instFree`, then
`Module.Invertible.free_iff_linearEquiv`).

This is the local input of Tag 0C0T's elementary route, at the generality the route needs: `S` is
the ring presenting `X ×_Y Spec 𝒪_{Y,t}` and `R` is `𝒪_{Y,t}`. -/
@[ega "I 2.4.8" "partial"]
theorem nonempty_linearEquiv_self_of_moduleFinite
    [Module.Finite R S] [Finite (MaximalSpectrum R)]
    (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M] [Module.Invertible S M] :
    Nonempty (M ≃ₗ[S] S) := by
  have : Finite (MaximalSpectrum S) := finite_maximalSpectrum_of_moduleFinite R S
  exact Module.Invertible.free_iff_linearEquiv.mp inferInstance

/-- **An invertible module over a ring module-finite over a local ring is isomorphic to the
ring.** The form of `nonempty_linearEquiv_self_of_moduleFinite` that Tag 0C0T's route consumes,
with the base local rather than merely semilocal. -/
theorem nonempty_linearEquiv_self_of_moduleFinite_of_isLocalRing
    [IsLocalRing R] [Module.Finite R S]
    (M : Type v) [AddCommGroup M] [Module S M] [Module.Invertible S M] :
    Nonempty (M ≃ₗ[S] S) := by
  have := finite_maximalSpectrum_of_isLocalRing R
  exact nonempty_linearEquiv_self_of_moduleFinite R S M

/-- **An invertible module over an algebra module-finite over `A` is generated by one element
after inverting a single element of `A` outside a given prime.** For `B` module-finite over `A`,
an invertible `B`-module `M` and a prime `p` of `A`, there are `g ∉ p` and `m ∈ M` with
`g · M ⊆ B · m`; since `M` is invertible, `b ↦ b · m` is then an isomorphism `B[1/g] ≅ M[1/g]`,
so `M` becomes free of rank one after inverting `g`.

Localizing `A` at `p` makes `B` semilocal, so `M` localizes to a free module there
(`nonempty_linearEquiv_self_of_moduleFinite_of_isLocalRing`); clearing denominators in a
generator and in the finitely many generators of `M` over `B` produces the single `g`.  This is
the spreading-out step that passes from a trivialization over `Spec 𝒪_{A,p}` to one over a basic
open of `Spec A`. -/
theorem exists_mem_primeCompl_forall_exists_smul_eq
    {A B : Type u} [CommRing A] [CommRing B] [Algebra A B] [Module.Finite A B]
    (M : Type u) [AddCommGroup M] [Module B M] [Module.Invertible B M]
    (p : Ideal A) [p.IsPrime] :
    ∃ g ∈ p.primeCompl, ∃ m : M, ∀ y : M, ∃ b : B, (algebraMap A B g) • y = b • m := by
  classical
  set T : Submonoid B := Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid B p.primeCompl with hT
  obtain ⟨e⟩ := nonempty_linearEquiv_self_of_moduleFinite_of_isLocalRing
    (Localization.AtPrime p) (Localization T) (LocalizedModule T M)
  obtain ⟨m, t, hmt⟩ : ∃ (m : M) (t : T), e.symm 1 = LocalizedModule.mk m t := by
    set z := e.symm 1
    clear_value z
    induction z using LocalizedModule.induction_on with
    | _ m t => exact ⟨m, t, rfl⟩
  -- every element of `M` becomes a `B`-multiple of `m` after multiplying by some element of `T`
  have key : ∀ y : M, ∃ (s : T) (b : B), (s : B) • y = b • m := by
    intro y
    have hz : LocalizedModule.mk y (1 : T) =
        (e (LocalizedModule.mk y (1 : T))) • e.symm 1 := by
      rw [← map_smul, smul_eq_mul, mul_one, e.symm_apply_apply]
    obtain ⟨b, s, hc⟩ : ∃ (b : B) (s : T), e (LocalizedModule.mk y (1 : T)) =
        Localization.mk b s := by
      set c := e (LocalizedModule.mk y (1 : T))
      clear_value c
      induction c using Localization.induction_on with
      | H x => exact ⟨x.1, x.2, rfl⟩
    rw [hc, hmt, LocalizedModule.mk_smul_mk] at hz
    obtain ⟨u, hu⟩ := LocalizedModule.mk_eq.mp hz
    refine ⟨u * (s * t), u * b, ?_⟩
    simpa [Submonoid.smul_def, mul_smul] using hu
  obtain ⟨G, hG⟩ := (inferInstance : Module.Finite B M).fg_top
  choose sg bg hsb using key
  set σ : T := ∏ y ∈ G, sg y with hσ
  obtain ⟨g, hgp, hgσ⟩ : ∃ g ∈ p.primeCompl, algebraMap A B g = (σ : B) := by
    have hmemT : (σ : B) ∈ Algebra.algebraMapSubmonoid B p.primeCompl := hT ▸ σ.2
    obtain ⟨g, hgp, hg⟩ := hmemT
    exact ⟨g, hgp, hg⟩
  refine ⟨g, hgp, m, fun z => ?_⟩
  have hsub : (⊤ : Submodule B M) ≤
      Submodule.comap (LinearMap.lsmul B M (σ : B)) (Submodule.span B {m}) := by
    rw [← hG, Submodule.span_le]
    intro y hy
    have hfac : (σ : B) = ((∏ x ∈ G.erase y, sg x : T) : B) * (sg y : B) := by
      rw [hσ, ← Finset.mul_prod_erase G sg hy]
      push_cast
      ring
    change (σ : B) • y ∈ Submodule.span B ({m} : Set M)
    rw [hfac, mul_smul, hsb y]
    exact Submodule.smul_mem _ _
      (Submodule.smul_mem _ _ (Submodule.mem_span_singleton_self m))
  obtain ⟨c, hc⟩ := Submodule.mem_span_singleton.mp (hsub (Submodule.mem_top : z ∈ ⊤))
  exact ⟨c, by rw [hgσ]; simpa using hc.symm⟩

end Stacks.RingTheory.SemilocalModuleFinite

end
